 "poem": "West Wind, bring to us a delicate wind\nFrom the boundless deeps of the ages behind,\nAnd breathe it upon the arid elevation\nWhere the white asphodel vegetation\nFalls over the stooping Meadow;\nThen, West Wind, bend down and drink from below\nThe spring's cold and liquid perspiration,\nWhen the comely willows can,\nAnd the long-vanished longlets fall again,\nAround your still summer snows.  Explain.\nWith our hands aloft again\nYou may throw upon heaven",
